'''Lake Iwarakí''' is a {{W|reservoir (water)|reservoir lake}} in the [[La Serra de Llevant]] comarca of [[Tramuntana Province]], [[Balboa]]. The lake covers an area of 704.586 km² and is situated in the northern highlands of the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range|Yalabirga]] range at an elevation of approximately 850 metres. The [[Gran Presa del Nord]], a major hydroelectric dam operated by the [[Corporació Hidroelèctrica de Balboa]] (CHB), is located on the lake's southern shore and regulates its outflow into the [[Yalabirga River]].

The lake's name derives from the [[Birú language|Birú]] words ''iwa'' (clear) and ''rakí'' (reflection or surface), meaning "clear reflections," referring to the still, mirror-like surface of the lake during dry season conditions. The name shares the ''rakí'' root with the Birú word ''warakí'' (peaceful water), reflecting the linguistic patterns of the indigenous Birú toponymy in the Yalabirga highlands.

== Geography ==

Lake Iwarakí occupies a natural basin in the northern highlands of the Yalabirga range, on the Shendan-facing (western) slope. The lake is fed by the Sant Jorge River and several smaller highland streams draining the surrounding ridges. The town of [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]] lies approximately 40 kilometres north of the lake, along the Sant Jorge River valley. [[Vila de Llevant, Tramuntana|Vila de Llevant]], the comarca capital, is situated on the eastern shore of the lake.

The lake was originally a natural freshwater lake formed in a glacial valley basin. Construction of the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] at the lake's southern outflow in the 1970s raised the water level and converted the lake into a regulated reservoir. The dam controls the outflow into the Yalabirga River, providing water storage for hydroelectric generation and regulated release for downstream agriculture and water supply.

The surrounding terrain is rugged montane highland, with forested slopes rising above the lake. The climate is cooler than the coastal lowlands, with a pronounced wet season from May to November that brings the majority of the lake's inflow.

== History ==

The area around Lake Iwarakí has been inhabited by the [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]] for centuries. The Birú maintained communities in the northern highlands following the Volisanian conquest of the isthmian coast and used the lake and surrounding highland valleys for fishing, agriculture, and seasonal settlement.

In the 1970s, the [[Corporació Hidroelèctrica de Balboa]] (CHB) identified the lake as a suitable site for a major hydroelectric dam as part of the post-reunification expansion of the Yalabirga hydroelectric network. The [[Gran Presa del Nord]] was constructed at the lake's southern outflow, raising the water level and converting the natural lake into a reservoir. The project was one of the largest infrastructure undertakings in Tramuntana Province during the 1970s and 1980s expansion period.

The dam's construction affected Birú communities in the area, contributing to the broader pattern of indigenous displacement associated with the Yalabirga hydroelectric expansion. Environmental groups and Birú communities subsequently opposed further hydroelectric development in the highlands, contributing to the political shift toward nuclear power in the 1990s.

